slipcase-open (0.1.5-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * macOS: built, and shipped as a notarized application bundle through the
    Homebrew cask excelano/tap/slipcase-open. A double-clicked container opens
    its payload, a save is written back, and the command line is linked into
    the Homebrew prefix. There is no menu bar item and no notification on this
    platform yet; sessions and recover are how to see what is open.
  * macOS reads policy from the two files Linux reads: /etc/slipcase/open.toml
    for the administrator, and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/slipcase-open/policy.toml for
    the person. Nothing installs either.
  * No change in behaviour on Linux or Windows.

 -- David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>  Mon, 07 Sep 2026 13:05:39 -0500

slipcase-open (0.1.4-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * A payload whose bytes are an executable image or a script, under a name
    claiming neither, is no longer opened. The check is the one the design has
    always described; what has changed is that it refuses rather than reporting
    and standing aside. It admits nothing — the allowed set still decides what
    may be opened — so a container refused here can be renamed and opened as
    what it actually is. The refusal happens before anything is extracted, so
    the payload never reaches the disk.
  * The refusal is said in whatever way the platform has that cannot be missed,
    which on a desktop with only notifications is a notification.
  * Windows: the tray icon takes a colour from what is happening — a payload
    that is a program, a save that did not reach its container, something
    waiting on a decision, or a save on its way home — and its menu says what,
    naming the file. The icon now stays until it is asked to leave.
  * Settings on Windows are read from the registry: machine and user policy from
    `SOFTWARE\Policies\Excelano\Slipcase\Open` under HKLM and then HKCU, over
    the user's own key. An ADMX and ADML pair ships as a separate administrator
    download, because a package runs no code at install and cannot place files
    in PolicyDefinitions. The setting names are the ones this file's package
    already uses.
  * Windows: sessions no longer accumulate directories that could never be
    removed. A packaged process asking for %LOCALAPPDATA% is given a redirected
    view of it without being told, and a directory belonging to the layer
    underneath that view cannot be removed through it; the session root is now
    asked of Windows rather than assumed. `slipcase-open policy` consequently
    reports the directory the packaged product actually uses, where before it
    named one it was not being given.
  * Windows: a refused container now says so on the first one of the day. The
    refusal is a message box, and the box was being drawn on a thread that the
    process then ended underneath — so a double-click with nothing else of this
    tool already running produced no document, no message and no window, and the
    same container refused loudly a minute later once something was running to
    hold the box up. It waits for the box now before it goes.
  * Windows: a refused container now leaves the tray icon red until somebody
    puts it down, which is what the icon's one reserved colour is for. The
    refusal was ending the process that would have raised the icon, so the
    colour only ever appeared when something else happened to be running.
  * Windows: whether an invocation gets a tray icon is now decided by whether a
    command line started it rather than by whether its error stream is a screen.
    A redirected command is still a command, and was being given an icon and a
    resident process it never asked for.
  * No change to the Debian package's behaviour: everything above is Windows,
    and the Linux layers, their paths and their spellings are unchanged.

 -- David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>  Sun, 06 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0500

slipcase-open (0.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * A `policy` verb. It reports which settings files this machine reads, what
    they resolve to, and where sessions and the front door live. The paths come
    out of the environment, so the file to edit and the file the documentation
    names are two questions and only the running program answers the first.
  * The manual page gains the front door and the desktop entry under FILES, and
    names the two user paths by their XDG variables rather than by the defaults
    they fall back to.
  * `--help` carries a footer naming both settings files. `-h` is unchanged.

 -- David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:30:00 -0500

slipcase-open (0.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Ship a Debian changelog. 0.1.0 and 0.1.1 had none, so there was no way to
    see from apt what changed between them. Debian policy makes its absence an
    error and this package had been failing that quietly.
  * Ship a manual page. slipcase-open(1) covers the four verbs, the two
    configuration files and their keys, and what the allowed set is and is not.

 -- David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:00:00 -0500

slipcase-open (0.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * A session announces its first write-back and then stays quiet. A
    notification on every save meant an hour's editing produced dozens of them
    about the same fact.
  * How much is said is settable, as `notify` in the policy file: "important"
    by default, or "everything" for the per-save notification. Nothing there can
    silence a question.

 -- David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 14:00:00 -0500

slipcase-open (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * First release. Open a container's payload in the application that normally
    handles it, watch for edits, and write them back.
  * Linux only. Windows and macOS are planned.

 -- David M. Anderson <david.anderson@excelano.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0500
